        </div>
      </div>
    ))}
  </div>

  <h2 id="portfolio">What a hireable generative AI portfolio looks like</h2>

  <p>
    Certificates rarely move the needle on their own in this field, because the underlying courses are
    widely available and easy to complete without ever building something that survives real use.
  </p>

  <div class="table-wrap">
    <table class="data-table">
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th>Portfolio piece</th>
          <th>What it needs to prove</th>
          <th>Common weakness to avoid</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr>
          <td data-label="Portfolio piece">Deployed RAG assistant</td>
          <td data-label="What it needs to prove">Grounded, source-cited answers over a real, non-trivial document set</td>
          <td data-label="Common weakness to avoid">A notebook that only runs on your laptop, with no live link</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td data-label="Portfolio piece">Evaluation write-up</td>
          <td data-label="What it needs to prove">You can define quality criteria and measure output against them</td>
          <td data-label="Common weakness to avoid">No comparison, no metric, just "it seemed to work"</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td data-label="Portfolio piece">Creative-AI campaign or asset set</td>
          <td data-label="What it needs to prove">Consistent, on-brief output plus your creative decision-making</td>
          <td data-label="Common weakness to avoid">Raw, unedited model output with no editorial pass</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td data-label="Portfolio piece">Agent or workflow demo</td>
          <td data-label="What it needs to prove">The system handles at least one realistic failure case gracefully</td>
          <td data-label="Common weakness to avoid">Only shows the happy path where everything goes right</td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <h2 id="india-reality">India hiring reality for generative AI roles</h2>

  <p>
    Indian job boards now list thousands of postings that mention generative AI, LLMs, or RAG, spread
    across product companies, IT services firms building client AI features, and a fast-growing set of
    AI-native startups. Entry-level generative AI roles are typically graded closer to a strong software or
    analytics hire than to a specialised research position, because most of them do not require training a
    model from scratch.
  </p>

  <p>
    Hiring managers across these postings repeat the same signal: they want evidence of a working, deployed
    system over academic credentials alone. A candidate who can walk through a real RAG pipeline they built
    and explain why it sometimes fails is treated as stronger than one who only lists a certificate.
  </p>

  <div class="vs-grid">
    <div class="vs-col vs-col--teal">
      <span class="vs-col__label">What actually helps in India right now</span>
      <ul>
        <li>A deployed project you can demo live and explain in detail.</li>
        <li>Domain knowledge from your current field, layered on top of AI tool skill.</li>
        <li>Comfort with fast tool changes instead of chasing one fixed toolkit forever.</li>
        <li>Willingness to enter through internships or a nearby existing role.</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
    <div class="vs-col vs-col--gold">
      <span class="vs-col__label">What quietly wastes time</span>
      <ul>
        <li>Collecting certificates without shipping anything that runs.</li>
        <li>Waiting for a job titled exactly like the course you finished.</li>
        <li>Learning every framework shallowly instead of one lane deeply.</li>
        <li>Assuming a single prompting skill is enough to compete for these roles.</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </div>

  <h2 id="salary-reality">Salary and pay reality for generative AI roles in India</h2>

  <p>
    Pay for generative AI work varies more by proof of shipped work and company type than by job title
    alone, but the broad bands below give a realistic starting picture for the Indian market.
  </p>

  <div class="table-wrap">
    <table class="data-table">
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th>Career stage</th>
          <th>Typical range</th>
          <th>What moves the number most</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr>
          <td data-label="Career stage">Entry-level, 0-2 years (LLM app dev, RAG, evaluation)</td>
          <td data-label="Typical range">Roughly Rs 6-14 LPA at product companies and AI-native startups; lower at IT services firms building client AI features</td>
          <td data-label="What moves the number most">A deployed, demoable project beats a certificate every time at this stage</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td data-label="Career stage">Mid-level, 3-6 years</td>
          <td data-label="Typical range">Roughly Rs 15-30 LPA, with fine-tuning and agent-engineering specialists at the higher end</td>
          <td data-label="What moves the number most">Ownership of a production system, including its cost and reliability, not just its accuracy</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td data-label="Career stage">Senior and specialist tracks, 6+ years</td>
          <td data-label="Typical range">Roughly Rs 30-60 LPA and above at product companies and AI-native startups, higher with equity at scaled startups</td>
          <td data-label="What moves the number most">A track record of systems that stayed reliable and cost-controlled in production, or product and safety leadership over AI features</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td data-label="Career stage">Non-technical lanes (safety, evaluation, GenAI product)</td>
          <td data-label="Typical range">Broadly in line with equivalent product, policy, or QA roles, often Rs 8-25 LPA depending on seniority and industry</td>
          <td data-label="What moves the number most">Domain expertise combined with AI literacy, not AI skill alone</td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <p class="section-note">
    These ranges are directional and change quickly as the field matures. Verify current figures against
    live postings for your city and company type before making a financial decision, and treat any single
    number you see quoted online as one data point, not a guarantee.
  </p>

